  "It isn't only pike now: a largemouth bass off the point may grab a bluegill you are reeling in, and from the boat a walleye may take a perch. Hang on, because the fight starts again with the bigger fish, right where the small one was.",
  "Saving a fish now says when it is the first of its kind or a new best, and warns when the same fish looks logged twice. Unsaved edits to a fish wait for you, search understands “yesterday”, “last week” and “Oct 15”, and Bests shows a calendar of your days out.",
  "A pike in the reeds may grab a golden shiner you are reeling in: hang on, because the fight starts again with the pike. And the landed card now counts a brook trout's darts you followed and a walleye's head shakes you eased off.",
  "The gear page keeps a record of the lures that took fish, packing lists copy as a checklist, and the Home card shows sunrise and how much is packed for today’s or tomorrow’s outing. Species pages show their newest photos, and several pieces of tackle can be added at once, split by commas.",
  "On the lake you can now see a walleye wag its head as it shakes, and a stuck catfish come partway up on the first pump. Both knock on the rod, and a phone or controller buzzes.",
  "A walleye now shakes its head once between runs: ease off a moment when the fight card calls it. And a sulking catfish takes two pumps to lift, not one.",
  "Bests can show one season at a time, outings show sunrise and sunset at the water, and a fish logged without an outing can make one of its day. Search understands “full moon”, “dusk” and “>3 lb 2 oz”, and backups carry the date in their name.",
  "Search results can be sorted largest first, and Share sends a catch with its photo. Bests and each water count how many hooked fish you landed, outings download as a spreadsheet, and the outing form can find the nearest water.",
  "A brook trout now darts: halfway through a run it cuts back the other way, so switch sides to keep turning it. And a largemouth bass that reaches the weeds or a dock piling stays buried longer, so turn its runs there.",
  "A rainbow trout's first leap is now a long tail-walk across the top: keep bowing until it's down. And at the inlet, turn every run upstream and the landed card says so.",
  "Miss the strike on a tiger muskie and it follows the bait: watch the float, because it hits once more with no warning nibble.",
  "At the inlet, the current now helps a run heading downstream take more line. Turn it upstream when the fight card calls it.",
